 "notes": [
  "Frozen from the numbers above by: node tools/bench/check-budgets.mjs --derive <fresh files>. Rule (also in RULE in the script): timings max = ceil(measured x 1.5 + 3 ms); download and wasm bytes x 1.05; wasm linear memory x 1.25.",
  "Observed run-to-run spread between the two chromium runs: at most 10% on medians above 5 ms, so 50% headroom catches a real slowdown (about 1.5x) and not a noisy neighbour; the check takes the best median of every fresh file passed.",
  "Not budgeted: whole-browser RSS (process tree, noisy, WebKit value misses helper processes), p95, HDDN numbers (comparison.json holds them), scanned-page numbers for any browser other than chromium and webkit.",
  "image-heavy render/page and scan repeat budgets are cached-render numbers (second and later draw); the first draw is budgeted separately and is the number to quote for a cold page.",
  "wasm bytes re-based (0.1.3, NEW-105/106): the 5% cap over the NEW-83 baseline (5,567,725 B) was used up by the 0.1.x engine fixes (0.1.2: 5,835,122 B, 0.1.3: 5,850,003 B, +14.9 KB for the font-twin glyph union and style segments). rawBytes and the two cold download budgets now use the 0.1.3 build as measured with the same x1.05 rule; no timing budget changed.",
  "wasm gzip -9 bytes re-based (0.1.4, NEW-113/110): the 0.1.3 gzip cap (2,232,320 B, still the NEW-83 baseline 2,126,019 x 1.05) was exceeded by 8,482 B (0.38%) by inline run styles (ADR 0013) and paragraph segments; 0.1.4 gzip is 2,240,802 B (raw 5,876,192 B, inside the 0.1.3 raw cap). Same x1.05 rule, no timing budget changed.",
  "exportBytes (NEW-83): Foliark native export size for the keyword flow of tools/bench/export-size.py (most frequent 4+ letter word redacted with a black fill), max = ceil(measured x 1.05). Deterministic bytes, no browser: `tests/oracles/.venv/bin/python tools/bench/export-size.py --check` after `cargo build --release -p foliark-engine --example export_size`. The table against PyMuPDF is in docs/performance/comparison.md."
 ],
